---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Solitaire & Card Game Glossary: Key Terms Explained | CardHearth';
const description =
  'A clear, beginner-friendly glossary of solitaire and card-game terms: foundation, tableau, stock, waste, build down, free cells, cascade, supermoves, redeals and more — explained in plain language.';
const updated = '2026-06-13';
const heading = 'Solitaire & Card Game Glossary';
const subtitle =
  'The vocabulary behind every patience game — defined plainly, so the rules and strategy guides make sense.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Solitaire & Card Game Glossary', href: '/guides/solitaire-glossary/' },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Solitaire & Card Game Glossary: Key Terms Explained',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Jared', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/solitaire-glossary/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/', label: 'Browse all games' }}
>
  <p>
    Solitaire games share a surprising amount of vocabulary, but the same word can mean slightly
    different things from one game to the next. This glossary defines the terms you'll meet across
    CardHearth — from the parts of the layout to the moves that win games — in plain language, with
    notes on where each one matters most.
  </p>

  <h2>The layout</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Foundation</strong> — the destination piles where you build a complete suit, almost
    always from Ace up to King. Clearing every card to the foundations is how you win in
    <a href="/klondike/">Klondike</a>, <a href="/spider/">Spider</a>,
    <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a> and most other patience games. There are usually four, one
    per suit (Spider uses eight).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tableau</strong> — the main playing area, the grid of columns where most of the work
    happens. You rearrange cards here to free hidden ones and assemble sequences before sending
    them to the foundations.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Stock</strong> — the face-down pile of undealt cards you draw from when you run out of
    moves in the tableau. In <a href="/klondike/">Klondike</a> you flip the stock into the waste; in
    <a href="/spider/">Spider</a> a stock click deals a new row across all columns.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Waste (Talon)</strong> — the face-up pile of cards turned over from the stock. Only the
    top card of the waste is playable. "Talon" is the older name for the same pile; the two terms
    are interchangeable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pile / Column</strong> — a single stack of cards. In the tableau these are usually
    called columns; "pile" is the more general word and also covers the stock, waste and
    foundations.
  </p>

  <h2>Cards and sequences</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Run / Sequence</strong> — a group of cards in consecutive rank order, such as
    9-8-7-6. Whether a run can be moved as a group depends on the game's building rules (see below).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Build down</strong> — placing cards in descending rank (a Seven on an Eight). Tableau
    play in Klondike, FreeCell and Spider is built down. <strong>Build up</strong> — placing cards
    in ascending rank (Ace, Two, Three…), which is how the foundations are built in nearly every
    game.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Alternating colour</strong> — a building rule where each card must be the opposite
    colour of the one beneath it (a red card on a black card). This is the tableau rule in
    <a href="/klondike/">Klondike</a> and <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Same-suit build</strong> — a building rule where cards must match suit to form a
    movable sequence. <a href="/spider/">Spider</a> uses this: you can always stack by rank, but
    only a same-suit run lifts as a single unit, and completing a King-to-Ace same-suit run removes
    it from play.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Face-up / Face-down</strong> — whether a card's value is visible. Face-down cards hide
    information; revealing them is usually the most valuable thing you can do. FreeCell deals every
    card face-up, which is why it's almost always solvable, while Klondike and Spider keep many
    cards hidden.
  </p>

  <h2>Special spaces and moves</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Free cell</strong> — a single-card holding space, the signature feature of
    <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a>. Each of the four free cells parks one card temporarily, giving
    you room to manoeuvre. The more free cells (and empty columns) you have open, the larger the run
    you can effectively move at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cascade</strong> — another name for a tableau column, especially in FreeCell, where the
    eight columns of overlapping face-up cards are called cascades.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Supermove</strong> — moving a whole sequence of cards in one action even when the game
    technically only allows one card to move at a time. It works because the program shuffles cards
    through your empty spaces behind the scenes. The number of cards you can move at once is
    <strong>(1 + free cells) × 2^(empty columns)</strong>: with all four FreeCell free cells open
    and no empty columns you can move five cards, and each empty column doubles that capacity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Deal</strong> — the initial layout of cards at the start of a game, and also the act of
    dealing more cards (as when you click the stock in Spider to deal a fresh row).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Recycle / Redeal</strong> — turning the waste back into the stock to go through it
    again once you've exhausted it. Some games allow unlimited redeals, some limit them, and some
    allow none. The redeal rule has a big effect on how winnable a game is.
  </p>

  <h2>Game features and helpers</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Auto-finish</strong> — a one-click action that sends every remaining card to the
    foundations once the outcome is certain, saving you the tedious clicks at the end of a won game.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Hint</strong> — a suggested legal move when you're stuck. Hints point out an available
    play but won't always pick the <em>best</em> one, so treat them as a nudge rather than a
    solver.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Seed / Deal number</strong> — the identifier that fixes how a deal is shuffled. Because
    every CardHearth deal has a number, the same seed always produces the same layout, so you can
    replay an exact hand, retry a different line, or share a deal with a friend to race the same
    cards.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few game-specific words are worth knowing too: <strong>peaks</strong> are the pyramids of
    cards you clear in <a href="/tripeaks/">TriPeaks</a> by playing cards one rank above or below
    the waste; <strong>matching pairs</strong> that add to 13 are the heart of
    <a href="/pyramid/">Pyramid</a>; and in <a href="/golf/">Golf</a> you build a single waste pile
    up or down by one rank regardless of suit, aiming to clear the tableau in as few "strokes" as
    possible.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Now that the vocabulary makes sense, the strategy guides will read a lot more clearly. Try
    <a href="/guides/how-to-win-klondike-solitaire/">How to win Klondike Solitaire</a> or
    <a href="/guides/freecell-strategy/">FreeCell strategy</a> next, and browse the
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> for the rest of the CardHearth library.
  </p>
</Article>
